:-) 3a.

But note that this is how I approach the question with an 
unspecified god.

When it comes to specific gods I admit various things regarded 
historically as gods exist, but don't regard them as divine [e.g. 
Planets, volcanoes, thunder, lightning, Roman Caesars, Japanese 
Emperors].

Others I regard as not existing because they are known to be 
fictional, or are logically inconsistent (in my view) [e.g. Binky 
the 900foot carrot god, all-loving gods that relegate unbelievers 
to eternal torture or that have other self-contradictory 
attributes].

Others I regard as so ill-defined that I can't judge whether they 
might reasonably exist or not [e.g. some versions of the Christian 
God, deistic gods].

Still others are totally unknown to me (e.g. I know little beyond a 
few names from the Hindu pantheon).

As far as I can see "I don't believe in gods" covers all these 
variations whereas none of the other positions do.

It would help enormously if there was a single definition of what a 
god actually is (or rather what something must be to be regarded as 
a god). The nearest I've ever seen is "A god is something that is 
believed to be a god" - which isn't very useful as a definition, 
but at least it covers all possible god-concepts. :-)

[Oh, and in this case I am 'godless' - I don't believe anything to 
be a god]

